# Bharathazhvar: A Proper Gentleman!

"How long can one keep eating hotel food? It is needless expense, there is no taste in the mouth, and one's health is ruined. Say what you like, but food at home is a pleasure of its own. No need even for vadai and payasam. Some fried dish, a chutney, rasam, and buttermilk are enough," Bharathazhvar said sorrowfully.

His words received sympathy from many quarters. Many agreed that Bharathazhvar was right and came forward to remedy his grievance. His marriage was conducted.

"Bharathazhvar will have no trouble now. His wife will lovingly serve home-cooked food, and he will eat, rejoice, and live happily," his friends said.

A few days after the wedding, Bharathazhvar returned to the Seema Hotel he had formerly frequented, carrying a vessel. First he ate there himself. Then he filled the vessel with food and went home, taking it "for his wife."

"What is this amusement? You used to weep that hotel food was bad for the body and too expensive. You said you were marrying to solve that problem. Now that you are married, not only do you eat at the hotel as before, you also carry food home," his friends mocked.

"What can I do, sir? She is weak and cannot work. I do not wish to trouble her. That is why..." Bharathazhvar said with a foolish smile and walked home.

You are laughing. You are saying this has happened in many places, are you not? Enough laughter. What I have told you is not merely a story. It is an affair in a high place. If those concerned learn that you and I are laughing at them, they will become furious and we shall be in trouble.

When I say a high place, I do not exaggerate. I mean the Government of India.

Though this country possesses abundant resources, facilities, materials, and intelligence, foreign rule diminished its industry. Raw materials were sent abroad, the country's workers were rendered helpless, and enormous sums were spent while the nation decayed, dependent upon foreign favour for every manufactured article. This was a terrible harm produced by foreign rule.

We gathered butter and gave it to an outsider, then begged him to give us ghee. Should this degrading condition not be abolished? Should our country's industry not grow? What could we not achieve? What article could we not manufacture?

Congress leaders repeatedly declared for many years: "Once foreign rule is abolished and self-government established, the disgrace of surviving through the mercy of other countries will disappear. We shall be able to manufacture in our own country, through our own people, the articles we need. Our country's prestige will rise, industry will grow, and expenditure will fall."

Self-rule has now blossomed.

On March 2, Comrade Bhabha, the Commerce Minister in the Government of India, answered questions in the manner of Bharathazhvar:

> This year, 7,895 tons of steel have been purchased from the United States.
>
> Seventy thousand tons of rails and related materials are being purchased from Canada at seventy-two dollars a ton.
>
> These goods have been purchased at a price higher than the price of the same goods in India because such materials are scarce here.

Is there much difference between Minister Bhabha's statement and Bharathazhvar's speech that I described? There is not, is there? But please do not make fun of it. This is an affair in a high place!

(*Dravida Nadu*, 21-3-48)
